# pregate-self-check's subjects (BL-878 item 7; BL-879 grammar). Every drive is in process: run.sh asks psc_main through inproc_run,
# sources the receipt as a library and runs blr_main in a subshell, and the self-check runs the seeder as svs_main in a subshell.
# The self-check and the seeder keep one command-line start per distinct CLI outcome (a refusal and a pass), started as
# FXCELL=<id> bash <subject> and compared on stdout, stderr and exit with the in-process answer on the same input. No subject needs
# an end-to-end cell: none relies on a process group, `$$` as a holder, exec of itself or an EXIT trap that would end the caller
# (the EXIT traps of psc_main and svs_main run in the subshell that holds them; the seeder's `exec < /dev/null` redirects that
# subshell only). The receipt has no command-line entry and no start.
cli scripts/pregate-self-check.sh cli-psc-refuse
cli scripts/pregate-self-check.sh cli-psc-pass
cli scripts/seed-verdict-store.sh cli-svs-refuse
cli scripts/seed-verdict-store.sh cli-svs-pass
# The self-check's own seeder starts (`bash "$SEED"`, byte-identical to the shipped script) are in process everywhere run.sh asks
# psc_main (its bash shim runs svs_main); the one cli cell cli-psc-pass runs the self-check as a program, whose seeder starts are
# real processes covered here.
nested scripts/pregate-self-check.sh scripts/seed-verdict-store.sh why=cli-psc-pass starts the self-check as a program and its own seeder starts (bash "$SEED") are real processes, which run.sh's in-process shim cannot route
